WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.900
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel 
and my name is Paul.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.920
Today I'm going to talk about the Turkish language

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.860
Now, right off the bat, the first thing I want 
to say is that there is a Turkish language.

00:00:22.219 --> 00:00:27.679
Because Turkey is situated at the edge of the Middle East, 
some people think that Turkey is an Arabic country

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.660
but it is not an Arabic country, 
the language of Turkey is Turkish.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.780
Although there is a small 
Arabic-speaking minority in Turkey.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:41.240
There are around 75 million native speakers of Turkish 
that includes 10 to 15 million people in Southeastern Europe.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:46.940
That's mainly in the part of Turkey that's located in Europe 
but also as a minority language in the Balkan area.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:53.340
And it includes 60 to 65 million people in Anatolia 
that's the part of Turkey that's located inside of Asia.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.540
Now those numbers are for Turkish.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:02.620
But there are other languages inside the Turkic language family 
that have a relatively high degree of mutual intelligibility

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:06.100
and some people consider those languages 
to be dialects of Turkish.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:10.760
So, if we include those, the number might be 
as high as 109 million people.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.720
But let's back up and look at the classification of Turkish.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:20.260
Because Turkey is located partly inside of Europe, 
you might have thought that Turkish is 
an Indo-European language or a Slavic language.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:25.620
and because part of Turkey is located on the edge 
of the Middle East, you might have thought that 
Turkish is a Semitic language.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.040
But it is none of the above.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.320
The Turkic language family 
is a separate family unto itself

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:36.260
The Turkic languages are sometimes thought to be a part 
of a larger language family called the Altaïc language family

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:42.840
which includes some other language groups 
like the Mongolic language family, the Japonic 
and the Koreanic language family amongst others.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:49.540
but this is not a generally accepted theory by linguists.
It tends to be debated and these days it seems to be fairly discredited

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.960
Turkish is part of the Oghuz branch 
of the Turkic language family.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.900
It also includes Azerbaijani which is
sometimes called a Azeri Turkish,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:02.760
as well as Turkmen as well as some other languages 
like Qashqai which is spoken in some parts of Iran.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:10.820
The languages of the Oghuz branch are all mutually intelligible
to some extent and these are the languages that are 
sometimes considered to be dialects of Turkish.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:19.800
Azeri Turkish is spoken in Azerbaijan as well
as parts of Iran, Iraq, Dagestan and Georgia 
and it has 26 million native speakers.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:29.940
Turkmen has 8 million native speakers including:
3.5 million in Turkmenistan, 2 million in northwestern Iran 
and 1.5 million in northwestern Afghanistan

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:34.500
All Turkic languages descended 
from a theoretical language called Proto-Turkish.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.080
 

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:42.480
The Turkic peoples originated in Central Asia and 
through migration they expanded over a wide area

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:51.040
extending all the way from Siberia 
down through China and Central Asia, through the Middle East, 
up into the Balkan area and even north of the Black Sea.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.980
Oghuz Turkic, the ancestor language of modern-day Turkish
language as well as all the languages of the Oghuz branch

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:02.860
was brought to Anatolia by the Seljuk Empire, 
a Turkic Empire in the 11th century CE.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:07.760
The Seljuk Empire had previously adopted Islam 
and they were admirers of Persian culture

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.960
and they were influenced by the Persian language 
and the Arabic language.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:16.360
Persian became the administrative and literary language
while Arabic was used for religious purposes.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.340
But Turkish was still spoken 
by the average common people.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:28.640
Later when the Ottoman Empire arose in place of 
the Seljuk Empire, the official language would be a form 
of Turkish that was highly influenced by Persian and Arabic.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:31.720
This form of Turkish became known as Ottoman Turkish

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.880
Ottoman Turkish was based on Turkish grammar but
with some influence from Persian and Arabic grammar,

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.620
but it was the vocabulary that was 
most influenced by Persian and Arabic.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:44.900
Sometimes up to 88% of the vocabulary
used was Persian and Arabic.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.600
This was the language of the elite people,
not the language of the common people

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.540
which had much less foreign influence,
although it did still have some foreign influence.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.340
When the modern republic of turkey was founded in 1923,

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:02.780
Mustafa Kemal Atatürk created the Turkish Language
Association to initiate a reform of the Turkish language.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.640
He essentially got rid of that administrative language,
Ottoman Turkish, and he replaced it with a new standard language,

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.280
based on the everyday common language 
spoken in Istanbul.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.700
And effort was made to replace Persian and Arabic words 
with native Turkish words.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:23.940
Sometimes, those were archaic Turkish words that were 
brought back to life and sometimes they were newly 
created Turkish words taken from Turkish roots.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:28.980
Another big changes that Turkish was switched from 
the Arabic alphabet, the Arabic writing system,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:34.120
to a modified latin alphabet which was much more
suitable for reading and writing Turkish.

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:40.080
Arabic has only 3 short vowels and 3 long vowels, 
while the Turkish language has 8 vowels.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:46.100
That created a lot of confusion in reading and writing 
in Turkish and the literacy rates had always been very low.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.020
But after switching to the Latin alphabet,
the literacy rate skyrocketed.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:54.980
but the common people haven't always accepted 
the suggestions by the Turkish Language Association,

00:04:54.980 --> 00:05:00.040
so some of those foreign loanwords in Turkish 
still exists alongside native Turkish words.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:04.560
Sometimes there are two words for the same thing, 
one Turkish and one Persian or Arabic.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:10.120
And they might have the same meaning
but just they're used in a slightly different way
or have a slightly different sense to the meaning.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:16.140
Before, I mentioned the Azeri language as well as 
the Turkmen language which are sometimes 
considered dialects of Turkish

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:22.500
but they are actually more similar to the Turkish that was 
spoken before the Atatürk reform of the Turkish language.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:28.780
That is to say that they use more Persian and Arabic loanwords 
than the modern-day standard Turkish of Turkey uses.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:35.660
And also Azerbaijan and Turkmenistan were under 
the control of the USSR for a long time. 
So there are also some Russian loanwords.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:40.680
And from what I understand, there are a lot of funny 
misunderstandings between the different varieties of Turkish.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:47.520
Azerbaijani or Azeri Turkish was always referred to 
as Turkish until the Soviet Union controlled Azerbaijan.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:54.780
And they changed the name of that language 
as a way of trying to create an identity for those people 
that was separate from the people in Turkey

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:57.240
That was another "divide and conquer" tactic.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:06.420
Just from reading some comments around the internet,
the level of intelligibility between standard Turkish 
and Azeri Turkish varies from 60% to 90%.

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:11.260
But even for the people who understand less of it,
it seems that they get used to it quite quickly

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.640
and it's simply a matter of familiarity 
and a little bit of adjustment.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:19.820
For example, one guy brought in his comment that, 
if a Turkish person takes an airplane to Azerbaijan,

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:25.000
maybe, in the morning, he won't really understand very 
well, but by the afternoon he will be completely fluent.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:29.560
And, if that is the case, then I would definitely say that 
those are just two dialects of the same language.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:32.440
So let's look at some features of the Turkish language.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:34.620
Turkish is an agglutinative language.

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:40.280
Agglutinative means that the words are comprised 
of pieces that all add to the meaning of the word.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:43.680
So, whereas in English, 
you might express something with a few words,

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:47.700
in Turkish, that might just be one word 
or it might be fewer words because

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:52.060
instead of using an extra word, you just add 
an extra piece to the word you're already using.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:55.840
That means you can sometimes get
some surprisingly long words in Turkish.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:58.540
Let's look at an example of agluttination.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:06:59.660
 

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:01.000
 

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:02.620
 

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:04.420
 

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:06.520
 

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:08.940
 

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:14.880
So you can see that if we add a piece to the end 
of the word, we're adding to the meaning of the word.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:22.540
This way of attaching suffixes to the end of a word
is also used to make plural forms, possessive forms 
and to show grammatical case for nouns.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:25.020
Let's look at an example for the plural form

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.200
 

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.080
 

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:32.340
Let's look at another example, with possession this time.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:33.780
 

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:35.600
 

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:37.000
 

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:38.780
 

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:42.620
Let's look at an example of the grammatical cases in Turkish.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:45.040
There are six grammatical cases  in Turkish.

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:51.760
And again, if you don't know what a grammatical case is, 
that means that the form of the noun changes 
depending on its function in the sentence.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:53.440
The word we're using is "tree"

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:54.640
 

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:56.180
 

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:57.900
 

00:07:58.440 --> 00:07:59.380
 

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:01.240
 

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:02.980
 

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:08.640
So you can see that the form of that noun changes 
depending on what function it fulfills in that sentence.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:10.160
How hard is Turkish to learn?

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:12.040
Well, that depends on your native language.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:17.040
If your native language is an agglutinative language 
or if you know another agglutinative language,

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:19.540
then Turkish might seem more familiar to you.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:25.080
But if you have never spoken a language like that before, 
you might have to get used to a whole new way of thinking.

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:29.220
But the good news is that Turkish grammar 
is very systematic and very consistent

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:33.060
And so is the writing system. 
Turkish is almost entirely a phonetic language.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:38.400
That means that for each sound, there is one letter. 
So when you read a word, you know how to pronounce it

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:40.760
And, when you know how to say a word,
 you know how to write it.

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:45.760
And, as always, if you're deeply fascinated with the culture, 
then it doesn't matter if the language is challenging,

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:51.000
because you'll be motivated enough to get past 
those challenges and you'll enjoy it every step of the way.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:55.520
So, if you happen to be very interested in Turkey 
or in Azerbaijan or Turkmenistan

00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:00.560
or other places where Turkish is spoken, then don't 
hesitate to start learning the Turkish language.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:02.680
Thank you for watching the Langfocus channel.

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:07.660
What should the question of the day be? I can't...
decide between a couple so i'll give you two choices.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:15.240
So people who speak Turkish or have studied Turkish,
do you think that Azeri Turkish and Turkmen are 
the same language? Dialects of the same language?

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.100
How well do you understand each other?

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:23.560
And another question that I'm curious about: Turkish people, 
if you try to read Ottoman Turkish from that period,

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:28.260
How well do you understand it? Are there too many loanwords, 
too much Persian and Arabic for you to understand?

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.960
Or is a close enough that you can understand it?

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:32.960
Let us know in the comments down below.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:37.420
Everyone else, comment as you wish.
